<!-- SECTION 1 -->
<div class="section-eyebrow">The zombie statistic</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">A number that circulates everywhere and means something different from what everyone thinks.</h2>

<p class="prose">The 80 percent figure for China's Malacca oil dependency has appeared in IMF working papers, Lloyd's market briefings, academic journals, and parliamentary security hearings. It is cited by commodity desks to support China's energy vulnerability thesis. It is used by accumulation underwriters to size corridor exposure. It is the foundation of a decade of geopolitical analysis on the so-called Malacca Dilemma.</p>

<p class="prose">It is a throughput number. It is not a dependency number. These are different quantities, and the difference between them is approximately 24 percentage points of China's crude import book.</p>

<p class="prose">The Narrows Commodity Dependency Model produces bilateral structural dependency figures: the share of each importer's total supply of each commodity that cannot realistically be sourced or routed without transiting a named chokepoint, accounting for bypass routes, alternative supply origins, and supply substitution lag. For China crude petroleum via Malacca, that figure is 55.8 percent.</p>

<div class="callout">
 <div class="callout-label">Throughput vs. structural dependency: the distinction that matters</div>
 <div class="callout-body"><strong>Throughput</strong> counts every vessel that physically passes through a strait. If a VLCC loaded in the Middle East transits Malacca en route to China, it contributes to the throughput figure. If it had instead taken the Lombok Strait, it would not. The 80 percent figure counts all the vessels currently transiting Malacca, including those that transit it only because it is cheaper and faster than the alternative, not because no alternative exists.<br><br><strong>Structural dependency</strong> asks a different question: what fraction of China's crude supply has no viable routing alternative to Malacca? A vessel that could reroute via Lombok at a cost of four extra days and $268,000 in voyage overrun represents an economic burden if Malacca closes, but it does not represent a supply halt. These are different risk exposures that require different reserve calculations.<br><br>The Narrows CDM models rerouting viability, bypass capacity, and alternative supply availability at the bilateral level for each importer-chokepoint-commodity combination. The 55.8 percent figure for China crude is the fraction that cannot be resolved without genuine supply disruption, not the fraction currently choosing the cheapest route.</div>
</div>

<hr class="section-rule" />

<!-- SECTION 2 -->
<div class="section-eyebrow">The bilateral routing map</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">Where China's crude actually comes from, and which chokepoints each origin actually requires.</h2>

<p class="prose">The throughput fallacy becomes visible when you look at China's crude import origin mix alongside its routing geography. China's crude supply comes from four broad origin clusters: Middle Eastern Gulf producers (Saudi Arabia, Iraq, UAE, Kuwait), Russia, West Africa (Angola, Nigeria, Congo), and smaller volumes from the Americas and North Sea. Each origin cluster has a different routing profile and a different set of mandatory chokepoints.</p>

<div class="compare-grid">
 <div class="compare-card featured">
  <div class="compare-card-label">Malacca-dependent origins</div>
  <div class="compare-card-title">Middle East Gulf crude: no exit without Hormuz, then Malacca or Lombok.</div>
  <div class="compare-card-body">Saudi, Iraqi, UAE, and Kuwaiti crude must exit the Persian Gulf via Hormuz. Eastbound to China, the standard routing continues through the Indian Ocean and either Malacca or Lombok. Neither route can be avoided entirely. For this traffic, both Hormuz and the Indonesian passages carry genuine structural dependency. The question is which passage, not whether a passage is required.</div>
 </div>
 <div class="compare-card">
  <div class="compare-card-label">Non-Malacca origins</div>
  <div class="compare-card-title">Russia, West Africa, Americas: no Malacca required.</div>
  <div class="compare-card-body">Russian crude arrives via the ESPO pipeline directly to Kozmino, or via Arctic routes, without transiting Malacca. West African and Brazilian crude arrives via Cape of Good Hope westabout, or via Suez/Red Sea, neither of which requires Malacca. These flows are counted in China's total crude import volume but they do not transit Malacca. Including them in a "Malacca dependency" figure is the root of the error.</div>
 </div>
</div>

<p class="prose">The structural dependency figure of 55.8 percent reflects this origin mix. It is not an underestimate of the physical traffic. It is the correct answer to a different question: what share of China's crude supply is structurally constrained by Malacca's navigability status, as opposed to merely currently transiting it?</p>

<hr class="section-rule" />

<!-- SECTION 3 -->
<div class="section-eyebrow">The bypass correction</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">Lombok handles 20.6 percent of China's crude. That traffic is exposed but not trapped.</h2>

<p class="prose">The Lombok Strait, 400 km east of Malacca, is navigable by laden VLCCs. It handles deep-draft vessels that cannot transit Malacca's 25-metre depth limit. It adds approximately four days to a voyage from the Indian Ocean to Chinese ports. At current Capesize day rates, that represents roughly $268,000 in voyage cost overrun per transit. That cost is real. The option to use it is also real.</p>

<p class="prose">The CDM shows that 20.6 percent of China's crude is currently routed via Lombok rather than Malacca. That traffic represents $74.4 billion in annual trade value and it is structurally reroutable away from Malacca because it is already using the alternative. If Malacca closes, this 20.6 percent of China's crude supply continues to arrive. It is not at Malacca risk. Including it in a Malacca dependency figure is double-counting the corridor's leverage.</p>

