<p class="prose">The table makes visible something that the 80 percent figure obscures: Hormuz carries higher structural exposure for China's crude book than Malacca does for a significant portion of the same cargo. Middle Eastern crude that currently transits Malacca first passes through Hormuz. Hormuz has no bypass. Malacca does. A risk model that sizes Malacca exposure at 80 percent may simultaneously be undersizing the Hormuz component of the same cargo's exposure.</p>

<hr class="section-rule" />

<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<div class="section-eyebrow">Where the 80 percent actually holds</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">For smaller SE Asian importers, Malacca dependency is structural and genuine.</h2>

<p class="prose">The CDM does produce very high Malacca dependency figures. They just apply to different countries than the ones most commonly cited. Vietnam carries 93.4 percent structural crude dependency on Malacca. The Philippines carries 95.6 percent. These economies have limited alternative routing, lower volumes of non-Malacca origin crude, and fewer bypass options in practical terms. For them, Malacca dependency is close to total dependency. A Malacca closure is a supply crisis, not a rerouting problem.</p>

