<p class="prose">The correct statement is not "Malacca is less important than we thought." Malacca handles extraordinary volumes of cargo and its disruption would cause severe economic harm. The correct statement is that China's exposure to a Malacca event is meaningfully lower than the 80 percent figure implies, and several smaller economies in the same region carry exposure closer to total that rarely appears in the same analysis.</p>

<hr class="section-rule" />

<!-- SECTION 5 -->
<div class="section-eyebrow">The LNG dimension</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">LNG via Malacca: 60.8 percent for China, and this component has no bypass.</h2>

<p class="prose">The crude figure comes with a bypass correction. The LNG figure does not carry the same relief. China, Japan, and South Korea all import Australian and South-East Asian LNG that transits Malacca southbound. LNG carriers cannot use Lombok as freely as VLCCs; the Strait's geometry and traffic separation scheme create practical constraints at LNG carrier drafts. More critically, the LNG source geography for Malacca-dependent imports does not have a Cape-routable alternative in the way that crude oil does.</p>

