<p class="prose">The LNG dependency figure for North Asian importers is structurally harder than the crude figure, for the same reason Hormuz LNG exposure is harder than Hormuz crude exposure: there is no supply substitution available at scale within a commercially useful time horizon. Australian and South-East Asian LNG that arrives via Malacca cannot be replaced by Atlantic Basin LNG with sufficient speed to prevent terminal drawdown and industrial disruption. The crude bypass correction applies to crude only.</p>

<hr class="section-rule" />

<!-- SECTION 6 -->
<div class="section-eyebrow">The accumulation implication</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">What the correct figure does to the reserve calculation.</h2>

<p class="prose">The practical consequence of the 80 percent figure is a systematic overstatement of Malacca accumulation exposure in models that use it uncritically. An underwriter sizing a treaty layer on Chinese crude exposure with an 80 percent Malacca dependency assumption is applying a structural factor 43 percent higher than the CDM bilateral figure. The implied PML is wrong in the same direction.</p>

<p class="prose">The correct framework treats Malacca exposure in three distinct buckets:</p>

<div class="cape-steps" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:var(--s3);margin:var(--s3) 0 var(--s4);">
 <div class="cape-step">
  <div class="cape-step-num">Bucket 01</div>
  <h3>Genuinely captive</h3>
  <p>Cargo from origins with no viable routing alternative to Malacca: certain South-East Asian loading ports, specific LNG supply chains. A Malacca disruption is a supply halt for this cargo. Full severity applies.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="cape-step">
  <div class="cape-step-num">Bucket 02</div>
  <h3>Economically captive</h3>
  <p>Cargo that could reroute via Lombok but at a cost that, below a certain disruption severity, makes transit preferable. At disruption durations over approximately 10 days, Lombok becomes rational. This cargo faces rerouting cost, not supply halts.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="cape-step">
  <div class="cape-step-num">Bucket 03</div>
  <h3>Origin-diversified</h3>
  <p>Chinese crude from Russian and African origins currently not transiting Malacca at all. A Malacca event does not affect this supply. It is not Malacca exposure regardless of how the 80 percent figure is applied.</p>
 </div>
</div>

<p class="prose">The Narrows CDM models these distinctions at the bilateral level. The 55.8 percent figure for China crude is the fraction in Buckets 01 and 02 combined. The fraction genuinely in Bucket 01, with no bypass option, is smaller still. An accumulation model that collapses all three buckets into a single 80 percent factor is mispricing risk in both directions simultaneously: overstating the severity of a pure Malacca event while potentially missing the compounded exposure that arises when Hormuz and Malacca stresses coincide.</p>

<div class="callout-verdict">
 <div class="callout-verdict-label">The model test</div>
 <div class="callout-verdict-body">Run the same corridor scenario with 80 percent and 55.8 percent Malacca dependency for China crude. The difference in implied 14-day VaR is approximately $34 billion at annual trade values. The difference in war risk PML at standard attachment rates is in the hundreds of millions. That is not a rounding error in a reinsurance treaty. It is a structural mispricing that the 80 percent zombie figure has been building into models for over a decade. The bilateral dependency layer is what corrects it.</div>
</div>

